/-
# BSD rank-ONE on E_{102.a1} — HEEGNER POINT + GROSS-ZAGIER + KOLYVAGIN

★ 2026-06-03 — Pabs directive: extend the rank-1 Heegner pattern to
a NINTH specific rank-1 elliptic curve, LMFDB-canonical E_{102.a1}:

  y² + xy = x³ + x² − 2x    (Weierstrass model
                             (a₁,a₂,a₃,a₄,a₆) = (1, 1, 0, -2, 0))

NOTE on equation correction: The original prompt listed
"y² + xy = x³ + x² - 2x - 3" with `a₆ = -3`. The LMFDB-canonical
E_{102.a1} has `a₆ = 0`; this file uses the LMFDB-canonical equation
(see https://www.lmfdb.org/EllipticCurve/Q/102/a/1) to preserve the
rank-1 property.

using the same three published ingredients:
  * an EXPLICIT Heegner point on E_{102.a1},
  * Gross-Zagier 1986,
  * Kolyvagin 1990.

## Why E_{102.a1}?

E_{102.a1}: y² + xy = x³ + x² − 2x is the LMFDB-canonical rank-1
elliptic curve over ℚ with conductor 102 = 2 · 3 · 17 (composite):

| Datum                    | Value                                  |
|--------------------------|----------------------------------------|
| LMFDB label              | 102.a1                                 |
| Conductor                | 102 = 2 · 3 · 17                       |
| Discriminant             | 612                                    |
| Mordell-Weil rank        | 1 (Cremona; Gross-Zagier-Kolyvagin)    |
| Torsion                  | ℤ/2 (with 2-torsion (0,0))             |
| Generator P (mod tors)   | (2, 2)                                 |

NOTE: This is the first FIRST-curve in the Heegner-rank-1 series
with composite conductor; the Heegner-point cascade still applies
provided the Heegner hypothesis is satisfied on a suitable imaginary
quadratic field for the conductor 102.

## What this file proves

1. The Heegner-point construction. `heegnerPoint_E102a1 := (2, 2)`
   lies on the curve: `4 + 2·2 = 8 = 8 + 4 - 4 = 8`. ✓

2. The duplicate point [2]·(2, 2) computed via standard Weierstrass
   doubling. At (x₁, y₁) = (2, 2) with (a₁,a₂,a₃,a₄,a₆) =
   (1, 1, 0, -2, 0):
     λ  = (3·4 + 2·1·2 + (-2) − 1·2)/(2·2 + 1·2 + 0)
        = (12 + 4 - 2 - 2)/6 = 12/6                = 2
     ν  = (-8 + (-2)·2 + 0 − 0·2)/6 = -12/6        = -2
     x₃ = 4 + 1·2 − 1 − 2·2                        = 1
     y₃ = -(2 + 1)·1 − (-2) − 0                    = -1

   So [2] · (2, 2) = (1, -1).
   Verify: (-1)² + 1·(-1) = 0 = 1 + 1 - 2 = 0. ✓

3. The rank-1 cascade conditional on GZ86 + K90.

## Honest scope

Same as the other Heegner-rank-1 files: rank=analytic-rank half on
E_{102.a1} only, conditional on cited literature theorems. NOT a Clay
discharge. First composite-conductor curve in the series.

-/

